      "title": "Using the Sum and Constant Multiple Rules for Differentiation"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "cf1fdaf7-e91e-502c-bb59-c2196955d4a6",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/problem": "Find $(\\mathbf{f}(t)+2\\mathbf{g}(t))^{\\prime}$, where $t\\ne 3$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}(t)&=\\begin{bmatrix}2 t^{3} - 1 \\\\ \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)} \\\\ - \\frac{2}{t - 3}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\mathbf{g}(t)&=\\begin{bmatrix}- t - 2 \\\\ \\cos{\\left(2 t \\right)} \\\\ e^{2 t}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Apply the **sum and constant multiple rules**:\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{f}(t)+2\\mathbf{g}(t))^{\\prime}=\\mathbf{f}^{\\prime}(t)+2\\mathbf{g}^{\\prime}(t).\n$$\n\nDifferentiate each component:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathbf{f}^{\\prime}(t)&=\\begin{bmatrix}6 t^{2} \\\\ - 2 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\left(t - 3\\right)^{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ \\mathbf{g}^{\\prime}(t)&=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ - 2 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} \\\\ 2 e^{2 t}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nScale every component by its constant multiplier:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1\\mathbf{f}^{\\prime}(t)&=\\begin{bmatrix}6 t^{2} \\\\ - 2 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\left(t - 3\\right)^{2}}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ 2\\mathbf{g}^{\\prime}(t)&=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ - 4 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} \\\\ 4 e^{2 t}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdd corresponding components and combine like terms:\n\n$$\n(\\mathbf{f}(t)+2\\mathbf{g}(t))^{\\prime}=\\begin{bmatrix}6 t^{2} - 2 \\\\ - 6 \\sin{\\left(2 t \\right)} \\\\ 4 e^{2 t} + \\frac{2}{\\left(t - 3\\right)^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nKeep the displayed inner arguments when applying the scalar derivative rules; any nonunit inner derivative multiplies its component.",
